export const SKILL = "---\nname: h3-prompt-writing\ndescription: Plan script-to-action timelines and normalize approved video prompts for MiniMax H3 T2VA, I2VA, FL2VA, and L2VA generation. Use when separating creative planning from H3 prompt formatting, composing integrated_multimodal_description, overall_soundscape, and non_diegetic_music, or aligning keyframes.\n---\n\n# H3 Prompt Writing\n\n## Visible performance versus deliberate stillness\n\nBefore accepting or reusing Layer 1, identify what a viewer can see change at the planned framing: a motivated gesture, attention shift, contact, reaction, or camera reveal of relevant information. Give that change a visible start and result. A fixed camera does not freeze the actors; quiet mood and stable identity/layout do not require a motionless performance. Background ambience, blinking alone, or an imperceptible drift is not a substitute for the shot's dramatic purpose.\n\nAn intentional tableau, still life, freeze frame or sustained pause is valid when its purpose and duration justify the hold. Do not require motion in every shot. Do not treat a legacy “nobody moves” instruction or the word “establishing” alone as that justification. If the canonical action explicitly forbids movement yet provides no coherent hold purpose, surface the planning conflict for upstream correction; do not quietly override it or invent new story events.\n\nKeep any added connective behavior inside the established action and end state. Do not perform the next shot's event early. A preparatory reach can stop beside an object; it must not become the next shot's push, transfer or pickup. Camera movement should reveal relevant information, not conceal missing performance.\n\nLayer 2 must carry the reviewed visible change into detailed action prose. Scope preservation to identity, costume and stable spatial relationships. Never replace a planned action with “remain still”, freeze mutable hands/gaze through retention language, or erase its visible result while shortening. For a deliberate hold, preserve the hold rather than adding compulsory gestures. This is prompt planning evidence, not proof of rendered video quality.\n\n## Two-Layer Operation\n\nThis skill has two layers that form one default production pipeline. For a structured shot rewrite, run Layer 1 first and persist its action timeline before Layer 2 writes the H3 prompt. A caller may request only one layer for inspection, but a final H3 prompt must never be produced from an unplanned shot.\n\n### Layer 1: Script-to-Action Planning\n\nUse this layer when the input is a script, storyboard, scene description, or\ncreative brief and another model needs a concrete video-generation plan.\n\nThe goal is to expose creative decisions before prompt writing. Produce an\n`action_timeline` that records the effective duration, subjects, objects,\nspatial anchors, camera states, sound, and timed physical actions. Each action\nmust use:\n\n`start_state -> actor/body part -> action/contact/path -> visible result -> exit_state`\n\nThe planning model may distribute a script's required actions across time and\nmay identify missing transitions, but it must not silently remove, simplify, or\nreplace a requested creative event. Treat a supplied duration as a reference\nunless the caller explicitly makes it a hard limit. Derive time from complete\nspeech, pauses, reactions and necessary actions before deciding shot count.\nIf content exceeds the caller's single-clip budget, split at complete speech\nturns or action results during planning; report the conflict in prompt-only work.\n\nLayer 1 output is planning data, not an H3 prompt. Recommended shape:\n\n```text\ncreative_intent: ...\nduration: ...\nsubjects_and_objects: ...\nworld_and_spatial_anchors: ...\naction_timeline:\n  - time: ...\n    start_state: ...\n    primary_action: ...\n    contact_or_path: ...\n    visible_result: ...\n    exit_state: ...\n    camera_visibility: ...\n    sound: ...\ncontinuity_contract: ...\nstate_ledger: ...\nwarnings: ...\n```\n\nLayer 1 may use the source language. It should use concrete action terms and\nexplicit time values, but it does not need H3 field names or English section\nprose. If another model will consume the result, pass the complete timeline,\ncontinuity contract, and warnings to that model without converting warnings\ninto silent edits.\n\nThe planner should keep 3–5 meaningful primary beats for a normal action shot, fewer for a genuinely simple shot, and one primary physical action per interval where possible. Do not create equal time bins or artificial micro-beats. This is a planning aid, not permission to reduce the action count. Complexity, ambiguity, and competing requirements belong in `warnings`.\n\n### Layer 2: Video-Prompt Normalization\n\nUse this layer when the input is already a video-generation prompt or a Layer\n1 `action_timeline`. The goal is to normalize expression for H3 while\npreserving the approved creative content.\n\nLayer 2 must:\n\n1. Identify T2VA, I2VA, FL2VA, or L2VA.\n2. Read the applicable guide under `references/`.\n3. Preserve actions, ordering, camera decisions, effects,\n   dialogue, subjects, and objects from the input.\n4. Add only the state, continuity, framing, or physical-link details needed to\n   make the approved actions unambiguous.\n5. Follow the canonical output contract below. Keep the official outer fields\n   and their order; inside the visual description, use chronological shot\n   blocks and natural timed paragraphs, not a fixed eight-block checklist.\n\nLayer 2 must not invent plot events, delete actions, turn a\nrequested effect into a different effect, or replace a difficult action with a\nsafer one. Any unresolved issue is reported separately as a warning.\n\nThe normalized H3 prompt is normally written in English, while dialogue,\nlyrics, and visible scene text remain in their original language. For structured generation, Layer 2 consumes the approved Layer 1 timeline; it may consume the source prompt directly only when the caller explicitly requests a Layer 2-only rewrite and supplies an already-authoritative action plan. The timeline and state_ledger are the source of truth for creative content. Preserve each resulting state and its duration of validity; normalization\nmust not turn a mutable state into a permanent identity trait.\n\n### Shared Scope Boundary\n\nThis skill is not a scriptwriter, director, video generator, or H3 capability\npredictor. It can structure and audit a plan, but it cannot decide the user's\ncreative tradeoffs. Physical continuity language may clarify a requested\naction, but must not become an unrequested creative decision.\n\nWhen a conflict is found, use:\n\n`[WARNING] requirement A and requirement B may conflict because ...`\n\nDo not use a warning to silently reduce action count, override a hard duration,\nremove a camera cut, or replace a requested effect.\n\n## Prompt Priority Order\n\nWhen requirements compete, use this order: (1) approved facts, exact dialogue and cross-shot continuity; (2) action and interaction that make those facts visible; (3) camera and sound choices that improve readability; (4) reference appearance and product detail; (5) style adjectives, which have the lowest priority. Preserve higher-priority content and simplify or remove lower-priority prose. Never add length to protect a style adjective.\n\n## Declarative Continuity Contract\n\nContinuity in the final prompt must be declarative. When an approved opening state exists or a preceding tail is explicitly selected as the first frame, state exactly what the shot must begin with and what each consequential object, person and spatial relation must do next. Do not use uncertainty or optional language such as “may”, “can reference”, “if available”, “try to”, “keep similar”, “尽量”, or “可能” for a required handoff. If the required reference or state is missing, stop before formatting and report the missing input outside the prompt; never weaken a required continuity constraint inside the prompt. A Ref2VA tail supplied only as optional continuity context does not become a mandatory first frame.\n\nThe opening state must be definite, the transition must be visible, and the ending state must be definite. Camera movement after the opening is allowed only when its path and purpose are stated; it must not replace the required opening composition.\n\n## Creative Core and Interface Wrapper\n\nWrite the action prose first as the creative core: a subject performs a physical action, the action affects another subject or object, the camera follows the information, and the visible result settles on screen. Only after that prose is coherent should it be placed into the required H3 fields. The six fields are an interface wrapper, not a reason to expose planning language.\n\nDo not put analysis, evaluation or author commentary in the final prompt. Avoid phrases such as “the main visible change is”, “this shows that”, “the relationship is”, “so the viewer understands”, or “the handoff remains unfinished” when the same result can be shown through visible action. Show the result through visible action. Replace interpretation with the image: a hand stops above a card, a gaze returns to the speaker, a shoulder settles, a prop stops under a palm.\n\n## Director Process Layer\n\nBefore formatting any H3 fields, describe the shot as a director-readable audiovisual process. This layer is mandatory for complex actions, product demonstrations, dialogue exchanges, and any shot that inherits a prior state; it is intentionally lightweight for a simple shot. The process is not another output schema and must not leak into the final prompt as a checklist.\n\nFor each consequential action, resolve this chain privately:\n\n`cause -> physical action -> contact or path -> visible consequence -> resulting state`\n\nThe resulting state is the handoff to the next action or the next shot. Carry forward the latest state of each important subject, prop, screen, garment, body posture, hand ownership, and spatial anchor. A new reference image supplies identity or an explicitly bound opening frame; it does not reset a changed state. If the previous shot ends with an object closed, held, removed, or moved, the next shot must open from that state unless an approved visible action changes it.\n\nUse detail where it prevents a likely visual mistake: weight transfer, hand contact, object path, eye-line, listener reaction, spatial crossing, product interaction, and the settle after the final action. Do not inflate simple actions into artificial micro-beats or bind prompt length to duration.\n\n### Interaction-first staging\n\nFor interpersonal action, resolve `initiative -> target cue -> response -> consequence` before writing the final prose. Show who initiates, what the other subject can perceive, how that subject visibly responds, and what state or action follows. For dialogue, keep the speaker, listener and visible response together; a line without an affected listener is incomplete when the scene depends on the exchange. Do not describe relationship with adjectives alone. Replace words such as cooperative, tense, hesitant or intimate with gaze, distance, hand movement, interruption, pause, turn-taking and the resulting change.\n\n### Coverage and sound decisions\n\nChoose camera placement and movement from the information the viewer must see. State what a pan, track, tilt, push, pull, or close-up reveals or preserves; do not add movement as decoration, and do not make a push-in the default. Keep the camera stable when stability best communicates a product state or physical action.\n\nDialogue is an exchange, not a line count. Preserve approved words and order, then show how the speaker delivers them, what the listener visibly does, and what action follows. Sound supports the event: use dialogue, room tone, handling sounds, impacts, or silence as appropriate. Music is optional; do not default to music or use it to cover an underspecified action.\n\n### Product and advertising staging\n\nFor a product shot, establish the product's identity and physical state, then show one readable interaction or transformation at a time. Connect the product benefit to a visible user action or consequence. Keep branding, controls, surfaces, screens, and scale consistent with the approved evidence; never invent a feature, interface state, or logo treatment to make the demonstration more persuasive.\n\nAfter planning, run two separate checks: (1) content and continuity — the action can be watched from cause to resulting state; (2) interface format — the required H3 fields, labels, timing syntax, and reference bindings are valid. Passing the second check does not imply the first.\n\n### Compact final-output rule\n\nKeep the director process internal. The final H3 prompt is a compact production brief, not the state ledger or a transcript of the planning pass. Include only facts that change what H3 must render. Do not repeat stable identity or global style in every beat. Keep the summary to one or two sentences; keep each subject definition to its identity, reference role and only the hard visual facts; keep retention analysis to consequential states and transitions; write the detailed action as a few causal beats rather than a full checklist. Put each sound layer in one concise sentence, and use `N/A` when music is not required. Remove redundant prohibitions after the positive staging is clear.\n\nThe compactness target for a normal shot is roughly 300–700 words across all six H3 sections; use fewer when the shot is simple and exceed it only when a real action or reference conflict requires the space. Use one canonical mention for each stable subject, reference role, global look, and continuity fact in its appropriate section. Do not copy the state ledger into the final prompt. In `detailed_description`, spend words on the action path and the changed state, not on re-listing identities already defined above.\n\n## Identity and mutable-state contract\n\n**Identity is not state.** Separate stable identity properties (face, hair, costume design, object geometry) from action-dependent properties (worn/removed, open/closed, held/placed, seated/standing). An accessory can define a character's usual appearance without staying worn throughout the clip. Reference images establish only the properties assigned to their roles; they do not reset an action's result.\n\nFor consequential changes, keep a compact `state_ledger`: entity, property, opening evidence/state, changing action and interval, resulting state, holder/location, and final visible state. Record only states that matter to the action; do not enumerate every background object. Carry each result forward until a visible, motivated action changes it. Occlusion, a cut or a new shot paragraph does not itself cause a transfer, duplication or return to the reference pose. Unknown states remain unknown, not guessed from a character sheet.\n\nFor object handling, retain the same object through contact, release and any transfer. Name the responsible hand and keep its object ownership consistent while the other hand performs another action. Sequentially stage actions that need the same contact area; overlap compatible actions normally. Fit mechanics inside flexible action windows, preserving the approved events, duration and hard timestamps. Do not solve overload by silently deleting an event or compressing several transitions into an unreadable instant.\n\nExample only: glasses begin on the face; the right hand grips and removes them; after removal the face is bare and the same pair remains in the right hand while the left hand rubs the nose and closes the laptop; the final view retains that state. A glasses reference preserves frame design, not permanent wear. Do not add a return-to-face action unless the story requires one. Apply the same reasoning to a released tool, a closed lid or a changed body posture, rather than inserting glasses rules into unrelated scenes.\n\nBefore returning the normalized prompt, compare identity definitions, preservation claims, action intervals and the ending against the ledger. Scope phrases such as “unchanged throughout”, “fully preserved” and “match the reference” to the specific stable properties they preserve. Repair the conflicting preservation sentence instead of appending more prohibitions beneath it. The ending reflects the last completed transition, not the initial reference pose. If approved facts themselves conflict, report the specific decision needed outside the executable prompt; do not choose a new story under the guise of formatting.\n\nThis is a writing check, not proof that the generated video is correct. Do not claim a tool enforces the ledger or that video review passed merely because this check passed.\n\n## Viewpoint and visibility contract\n\nStory facts are not automatically visible frame content. For each planned view, check camera position -> subject orientation -> visible surfaces -> occlusion -> readable detail. Separate what exists in the story, what a character can see, what the camera can see and what the viewer can actually read at this framing. Apply this distinction across definitions, summary, preservation claims and action prose, not only at a subject's first appearance.\n\nDescribe only supported visible details as on-screen content. A rear-facing display may illuminate a face without exposing its interface to the camera; a hidden inscription may motivate an action without becoming readable. Keep necessary unseen facts as explicitly off-camera context, or omit them from visual prose when they add no staging information. Do not turn such facts into a screen insert, overlay or full-frame graphic. These are general visibility examples, not a ban on screens, text or close-ups.\n\nDo not invent a cut, reverse angle, transparent surface or object rotation merely to reveal hidden information. Layer 1 may plan a motivated reveal when the creative brief permits coverage choices; Layer 2 must preserve the approved camera plan and opening reference. If an explicitly required visible detail cannot be shown from that plan, report the specific visibility conflict outside the executable prompt instead of silently changing the shot. Uncertain or unseen image details remain unspecified; a file path or reference label is not visual evidence.\n\nSeparate the reference subject from the presentation of its reference image. Identity/design references contribute the assigned person's or object's properties, not contact-sheet panels, white backing, captions, browser chrome or multi-view layout. Multiple views resolve to one physical subject, not a collage inside the scene. If a reference is explicitly assigned a layout or composition role, preserve that role rather than globally stripping its layout. An accepted scene tail supplies the opening view; an identity sheet must not replace that view.\n\nBefore delivery, compare each claimed visible surface and readable detail with the approved camera plan and inspected references. Rewrite the source claim wherever it conflicts; do not append a prohibition while retaining incompatible visual prose. Keep this check internal and retain the existing output schema. Textual consistency does not certify reference delivery or generated-frame correctness.\n\n## Cross-section ambiguity resolution\n\nBefore formatting, resolve consequential claims by entity, property, time interval, reference role and evidence. Distinguish a real contradiction (incompatible values for the same property at the same time) from a motivated change over time or two references assigned different roles. Unknown evidence is not permission to invent a state. Preserve explicit approved events and inspected opening evidence; if they materially conflict, expose the specific conflict outside the executable prompt rather than silently choosing either.\n\nDo not mix stable properties and mutable states in one blanket preservation claim. Describe identity/design separately from location, ownership, posture, contact and on/off or open/closed state. Qualify an initial state as initial; scope a continuing claim to its actual interval. A preservation marker alone does not resolve a contradiction: changing fully_preserved to partially_preserved while retaining an overbroad sentence is insufficient.\n\nAudit all applicable output sections together, including subject_definitions, summary, retention_analysis, detailed_description and sound sections, against the same approved timeline. Check reference labels and pronoun referents, object count and hand ownership, support and position, key light versus sole light source, camera translation versus pan/tilt, and on-screen speech versus post-production narration. For example, a stationary camera position can allow a specified tilt, but a completely locked orientation cannot; a key light can coexist with screen fill, but a sole light source cannot. These are interpretation examples, not mandatory scene content or bans on movement.\n\nRewrite or remove the conflicting source sentence in every affected section; do not leave it intact and append an exception, negative prompt or repeated prohibition. Use the smallest positive description that makes the intended state and transition unambiguous. Recheck the ending against the last completed action and make sure the repair has not changed approved dialogue, duration, hard timestamps, reference roles or input mode. If action capacity or timing truly cannot satisfy the approved plan, report that conflict rather than inventing an extra action or silently dropping an event.\n\nLayer 1 owns the action/state plan; Layer 2 preserves that plan while resolving wording and normalizing format, not recreating the story or resetting states to reference appearances. Keep this audit internal: emit only the required output schema, not extra ledger/checklist sections or a claim of visual validation. This is a writing check, not proof that the generated video is correct.\n\n## Workflow\n\n### Natural Timing\n\nA reference duration is adjustable, including during prompt revision. Estimate\ncomplete dialogue at a natural pace for its language, delivery and emotion;\ninclude breaths, punctuation pauses, turn handoffs and relevant listener\nreactions. Word/character counts are rough planning estimates, not universal\nspeech-rate targets. Prefer measured speech duration when available.\n\nRecompute in both directions: shorten an oversized reference as readily as\nextending an insufficient one. First-frame composition, appearance and actions\nalready completed at the opening are states, not additional timed beats;\ndescription length is not playback time. A long silent hold needs a supported\ndramatic or visual purpose. A reference or text estimate above the clip budget\nis not itself proof that the complete events cannot fit.\n\nListening, gestures, camera movement and ambience may overlap speech where\nplausible. Add only actions and pauses that need separate time. Reflow flexible\nwindows without altering exact words, event order or opening/ending states.\nNever accelerate, truncate or paraphrase speech to satisfy a reference target,\nand never invent events merely to fill an extended clip. Update effective\nduration, all time windows and last-keyframe alignment together. If the caller\nrequires exact timing, surface an infeasible plan rather than rushing it.\n\n1. Default to the pipeline `Layer 1 -> persist action_timeline -> Layer 2`; expose the intermediate plan in the execution record, not inside the final H3 prompt.\n2. For Layer 1, extract the creative requirements and construct the\n   `action_timeline`; keep it compact and causal, then validate duration, coverage and final state before saving.\n3. For Layer 2, require the saved timeline, identify the input mode and read the applicable H3 guide.\n4. Preserve the exact field names, section order, labels, and timing notation\n   from the selected guide.\n5. Separate creative requirements from execution constraints before editing.\n6. Validate continuity and report unresolved warnings outside the prompt.\n\n### Dialogue Placement\n\nDialogue is event data, not a detached list that the model must redistribute.\nKeep the output layout flexible, but place each complete spoken turn inside the\nchronological action passage where it occurs. The passage should identify the\nspeaker, stable voice ID, approximate time or ordering cue, delivery, exact\nline, and the relevant silent response before moving to the next turn. A shot\nwithout explicit time windows must still make the order and handoff unambiguous.\n\nUse one stable voice ID for one physical speaker across the prompt and across\nshots. Do not assign a voice ID to a silent person, a background crowd, or a\nvisual group unless the group actually speaks together; a visual group and an\nindividual member who speaks are separate mappings. Resolve titles, names and\nforms of address to the existing physical subject rather than creating another\nspeaker. Keep a separate dialogue catalogue only when the transport contract\nrequires it; it must not contradict the event-led dialogue.\nAn individually mapped group member remains the same already-present person,\nnot an extra body beside the group. For collective speech, use the participating\nspeakers' compound IDs rather than inventing a second identity for the group.\nKeep action narration consistent with the actual turns: speaker names, question\ncounts and handoffs must agree with the supplied lines, without implying extra\nintelligible speech.\n\n### Deterministic Artifact Validation\n\nFor machine-produced artifacts, validate before sending the prompt to H3:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/validate.py \\\n  --timeline path/to/action-timeline.json \\\n  --prompt path/to/h3-prompt.json \\\n  --mode FL2VA\n```\n\nThe validator checks the Layer 1 contract, interval coverage, gaps, overlaps,\nduration, required physical-action fields, H3 core-field order, and keyframe\nalignment markers. It does not judge creative quality or claim that H3 will\nexecute the prompt correctly. The contracts are documented in\n`schemas/action-timeline.schema.json` and `schemas/h3-prompt.schema.json`.\nRun the validator from the skill directory, or use an absolute path to the\nscript. A failed validation is a blocking artifact error; a `[WARNING]` in the\ntimeline is a creative review item and must not be silently removed.\nOlder timeline artifacts created before this contract may fail because they\nlack `camera_visibility` or `sound`; migrate those fields explicitly before\npassing the artifact to Layer 2.\n\n## H3 Input Modes (Layer 2)\n\n- T2VA: build the full audiovisual timeline from text.\n- I2VA: start from the first frame and develop forward from it.\n- FL2VA: describe the continuous path between the first and last frames.\n- L2VA: infer a plausible opening and converge to the supplied last frame.\n\nUse `integrated_multimodal_description`, `overall_soundscape`, and `non_diegetic_music` in the order shown in `references/base-en.txt`.\n\n## Output Rules\n\n- The final Layer 2 artifact must use the canonical format below; free-form or\n  partial envelopes are not valid new outputs. Legacy prompts remain source\n  material, not alternative output formats.\n- Write rewrite sections in English; preserve dialogue, lyrics, and visible scene text in their original language.\n- Describe each shot by composition, subjects, environment, actions, camera, sound, and the exact point where referenced content appears.\n- Avoid plot summaries, unresolved reference labels, and timing that does not match the effective duration.\n\n## Canonical Output Contract\n\nThe field definitions live in `references/output-format.json`, shared by the\nworkshop runtime and `scripts/validate.py`. Official mode-specific envelopes\nare preserved; all modes share the same chronological body conventions.\n\n- T2VA / I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA: exactly `integrated_multimodal_description`,\n  `overall_soundscape`, `non_diegetic_music`, in that order. Add only the\n  selected guide's official keyframe instruction before the fields; T2VA has none.\n- Ref2VA: route to `../h3-ref2va-prompt-writing/SKILL.md`. Its exact fields are\n  `subject_definitions`, `summary`, `retention_analysis`, `detailed_description`,\n  `overall_soundscape`, `non_diegetic_music`, in that order. This is the same\n  reference contract documented in `references/ref-en.txt`.\n- Write each lowercase field once at the start of a line. Give every field\n  content; use `N/A` for absent ambience or score. No Markdown fences or\n  diagnostics in the usable prompt. A caller's internal duration metadata is\n  removed before saving and submission.\n- Start the visual body with `[Shot 1]`, including a single continuous take.\n  Introduce `[Shot 2]`, `[Shot 3]`, etc. only for actual cuts, without gaps or\n  duplicates; put each later cut time at the beginning of its block.\n- Within a shot, use meaningful `MM:SS.mmm-MM:SS.mmm` event windows. The\n  opening paragraph establishes the starting composition; the last action\n  paragraph reaches the approved end state. Multiple timed beats are not cuts.\n  Do not replay the event in an extra summary, state ledger or dialogue list.\n- Emit each scripted speech turn beside its sole speaker and stable `(Sx)`\n  inside the chronological body, with original words in `<d>[language] ...</d>`.\n  Intentional repeated turns stay repeated. Whole-clip sound and music fields\n  do not repeat speech. Detail remains proportional to event complexity.\n\n## Generic Block Labels\n\nThe labels below are planning vocabulary, not a second final-output template.\nUse them when organizing Layer 1 facts; translate their relevant information\ninto the canonical Layer 2 shot paragraphs. Do not append eight separate\nledgers after the visual body. Natural paragraphs and timed passages remain\nvalid inside the required outer envelope.\n\nPreferred labels are:\n\n- `[REFERENCE_ALIGNMENT]`\n- `[SUBJECTS]`\n- `[WORLD_CONTINUITY]`\n- `[OBJECT_CONTINUITY]`\n- `[ACTION_ENGINE]`\n- `[SHOT_1]`, `[SHOT_2]`, `[SHOT_3]` or the required shot sequence\n- `[STATE_TRANSITION]`\n- `[CAMERA_LOGIC]`\n- `[AUDIO_LOGIC]`\n- `[RESTRICTIONS]`\n\nDo not use scene-specific labels such as `[SPELL]`, `[TAKEOFF]`, `[WUXIA_COMBAT]`, `[ELDER_VIEW]`, or `[LANDING]` as reusable template sections. Put those details inside `[ACTION_ENGINE]`, `[STATE_TRANSITION]`, or the relevant `[SHOT_N]` paragraph. For example, describe a jump, spell, vehicle movement, transformation, or impact as a concrete action within the timeline rather than naming the whole block after that action.\n\nKeep the reusable template generic even when the current example is genre-specific. H3 should receive the scene-specific visual semantics in the prose, while the section structure remains stable across live action, animation, combat, dialogue, movement, and ordinary environment shots.\n\n## Event-Led Shot Writing\n\n### Reference Economy\n\nPlan separate reference images only when recognizable appearance must persist\nacross shots or the viewer needs to inspect or identify the object. Repetition,\nmention in the script, or ordinary handling alone is not sufficient. Keep\ngeneric incidental objects in text and preserve their actions and states.\nReuse appearance already covered by a scene anchor, character sheet or product\nimage rather than splitting furnishings, clothing and accessories into more\nassets. A one-off focal clue or product detail may still justify a reference.\nDo not use object-name blacklists or a fixed image quota. Preserve explicit\nuser references and existing approved assets; prompt-only rewriting uses the\nactual supplied list and does not silently remove assets or request new images.\n\nWrite what the audience sees and hears as the event unfolds, not a compliance\nchecklist. Establish only the starting composition needed to read the event,\nthen develop it toward the approved visible result or intentional hold.\n\nDetail follows complexity, not character quotas or duration-based minimums.\nFor meaningful physical changes, connect cause, action, contact or path,\nconsequence, and the changed state. For dialogue, preserve exact words and\nownership, convey delivery, and show relevant listening or response. A short\nline can carry a full beat; a silent shot does not need invented speech.\n\nCamera work serves the event or information reveal. Honor explicit choices;\nwhere unspecified, choose coherent framing, movement, focus, or cuts rather\nthan defaulting to a push-in. Timing follows actions, pauses, speech, and\nreactions; these may overlap, and timed paragraphs do not imply cuts.\n\nExpress style through medium, materials, light, movement, recording texture,\nand sound, not adjective stacks. State identity and continuity anchors once,\nthen describe changes. Do not add duplicate summaries, exhaustive body-part\ninventories, or prevention lists to pad a simple shot. Preserve explicit user\nconstraints concisely; keep authoring diagnostics outside the usable prompt.\n\n## Physical-Action-First Planning\n\nWhen a prompt contains manipulation, contact, impact, locomotion, or a state change, prioritize executable physical action over mood adjectives or plot summary. Resolve requirements in this order:\n\n1. Physical action and contact visibility\n2. Subject, object, and spatial continuity\n3. Camera placement and framing needed to show the action\n4. Narrative information and emotional reaction\n5. Style and mood language\n\nEach timed action block must state four things: the starting state, the visible action, the physical connection or motion path, and the ending state. The ending state of one block must be the starting state of the next block. Do not compress a multi-step manipulation into a result phrase such as `removes and cleans the glasses` when the intermediate states matter.\n\nFor each hand-operated object, define the total number of arms and hands, the responsibility of each hand, the object-to-hand attachment, the action area, and the camera framing. Keep the object and the responsible hand visibly connected until a transfer is complete. For contact actions, state what touches what and what does not touch. Do not solve a structural ambiguity with a longer negative list.\n\nAllocate time according to physical complexity, not only story importance. A single tap may take a short interval; removal, wiping, collision, release, transfer, landing, or other multi-step actions require separate intervals and readable intermediate states. The next action may overlap in momentum, but it must not erase the previous action's exit state.\n\n## Timeline Validation\n\nBefore finalizing a prompt, validate the timeline:\n\n- The intervals cover the effective duration without unexplained gaps.\n- Every interval has an explicit start state and exit state.\n- Adjacent intervals agree on subject position, object state, hand ownership, contact, and camera visibility.\n- Each interval has one primary physical action; secondary reactions must not compete with it.\n- Key contact points remain inside the frame and are not hidden by a crop, close-up, hand, clothing, or foreground object.\n- A prop that is not required by the current request is excluded from both the positive description and the scene setup.\n- Negative constraints are short and only reinforce a positively specified action.\n\nThe deterministic validator enforces the structural subset of these checks.\nSemantic continuity (for example, whether a hand description really matches\nthe intended prop) still requires human or model review.\n\nTimeline validation checks internal consistency; it is not a quality prediction\nor a reason to rewrite the user's action plan. If the source contains several\nactions in one interval, preserve them and report the density separately. If\ntwo requirements conflict, preserve both in the prompt only when they can be\nrepresented without contradiction; otherwise report the exact conflict and\nask for a creative decision rather than choosing one silently.\n\nWhen reporting a concern, use this form outside the prompt:\n\n`[WARNING] requirement A and requirement B may conflict because ...`\n\nDo not use warnings to justify silently reducing action count, overriding a\nhard duration, removing a camera cut, or replacing a requested effect.\n\nFor a high-risk action, perform a final audit using this compact schema:\n\n`start_state -> actor/body part -> contact or path -> visible result -> exit_state`\n\nUse this schema for combat contact, object handling, screen interaction, wearing or removing items, and any action where H3 could skip an intermediate state.\n## Tips for Better Results\n- Match the description to the effective video length and the caller's supported single-clip budget; a reference target is not a hard cap.\n- Keep reference labels consistent (e.g. `<Picture 1>`, `<Video 1>`, `<Audio 1>`) across every section.\n- Prefer concrete visual and audio details over abstract words like \"cinematic\" or \"beautiful\".\n- When using keyframes (I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA), clearly state how the first and/or last frame connects to the timeline.\n";

export const BASE_EN = "# Video Prompt Writing Guide (T2VA / I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA)\n\n## 1. Task Overview\n\n- **T2VA**: Builds a complete audiovisual timeline from text.\n- **I2VA**: T2VA body + first-frame instruction + a visual path that develops forward from the first frame.\n- **FL2VA**: T2VA body + first-and-last-frame instruction + a continuous path from the first frame to the last frame.\n- **L2VA**: T2VA body + last-frame instruction + a path that converges from a plausible preceding state to the last frame.\n\n## 2. Final Prompt Structure\n\n### 2.1 Part One Is the Instruction\n\n**T2VA** has no image-alignment instruction and begins directly with the three core fields.\n\n**I2VA** always uses:\n\n```text\nFor the target video, at 0.00 seconds into the target video, <Picture 1> (from [Shot 1]) is fully referenced.\n```\n\n**FL2VA** always uses:\n\n```text\nHow the reference pictures align with the target video — Picture 1 (from Shot 1) aligns with the 0.00-second mark of the target video; Picture 2 (from Shot N) aligns with the S.SS-second mark of the target video.\n```\n\n**L2VA** always uses:\n\n```text\nHow the reference pictures align with the target video — <Picture 1> (from [Shot N]) aligns with the S.SS-second mark of the target video.\n```\n\nHere, `N` is the index of the actual final shot, and `S.SS` is the effective video duration formatted to exactly two decimal places. The instruction must be the first line of the final prompt, followed by one blank line before the core fields.\n\n### 2.2 Part Two Contains the Three Core Fields\n\n```text\nintegrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] ...\n\noverall_soundscape: ...\n\nnon_diegetic_music: ...\n```\n\n- **integrated_multimodal_description**: Describes visuals, actions, shots, speakers, dialogue, singing, and diegetic audio along the timeline.\n- **overall_soundscape**: Summarizes ambient sound, physical action sounds, and non-verbal human sounds across the entire video.\n- **non_diegetic_music**: Describes background music that the characters cannot hear and only the audience can hear.\n\nThese three official fields are required once each, in this exact order, at\nthe start of a line. Every field has content; use N/A for absent sound or score.\nThe canonical definition is in output-format.json. Start the integrated body\nwith [Shot 1], even for a single take; add consecutive [Shot N] labels only for\nactual cuts, with each later cut time at the beginning of its block. Use\nmeaningful MM:SS.mmm-MM:SS.mmm event windows inside each shot; time windows\nalone do not imply cuts. Eight internal subheadings are not required. Write an\nunfolding audiovisual event, not a filled checklist. Detail follows action\ncomplexity and expressive purpose, with no duration-based minimum character\ncount. Define relevant identities and opening facts once, then describe\nchanges, exact speech and listener reactions, motivated camera work, and\nsynchronized sound. The last action paragraph contains the approved ending.\n\n## 3. How to Incorporate Keyframes into the Multimodal Description\n\n### 3.1 I2VA: Begin from the Image and Develop Forward\n\n`<Picture 1>` is the actual first frame of the video at 0.00 seconds and belongs to `[Shot 1]`. The description should first establish the style, subjects, composition, and scene anchors in the image, then describe the next action. Character identity, clothing, colors, key objects, and spatial relationships should remain consistent.\n\nRecommended structure: **first-frame anchor → action onset → continuous development → result or reaction**.\n\n### 3.2 FL2VA: Describe the Path Between the First and Last Frames\n\nPicture 1 is the opening, and Picture 2 is the ending. Focus on how the subject moves, how poses change, how objects are manipulated, how the composition evolves, and how the scene or lighting transitions.\n\nFL2VA generally favors a single shot so the model can interpolate continuously from the first frame to the last frame. Use multiple shots only when they are explicitly specified. The last frame must be reached by the final `[Shot N]` at the end of the video.\n\nRecommended structure: **first-frame state → observable intermediate changes → progressively narrowing differences → last-frame state**.\n\n### 3.3 L2VA: Infer the Opening and Land on the Image at the End\n\n`<Picture 1>` is the final frame of the video and belongs to the last `[Shot N]`; it does not inherently belong to Shot 1. Infer a plausible earlier state from the user's intent and the last frame, then describe how the characters, objects, camera, and scene gradually approach the reference image.\n\nRecommended structure: **plausible preceding state → explicit action and transition path → gradual convergence in the final shot → last-frame landing**.\n\n## 4. How to Write the Three Shared Core Sections\n\n### 4.1 Develop the Multimodal Description Along the Timeline\n\n`integrated_multimodal_description` is the main body of the rewritten prompt. Every detail should correspond to something visible or audible: visual style, initial composition, subject appearance and position, scene and key props, actions and reactions, shot changes, spoken language, and synchronized diegetic sound.\n\nAt the beginning of `[Shot 1]`, state the overall style and initial composition. Common styles include `Cinematic`, `live-action`, `2D-animated`, `3D CG`, `claymation`, `watercolor`, and `vintage film`. For keyframe tasks, derive the style from the reference image; for T2VA, select it from the user's text.\n\n```text\n[Shot 1] Live-action, cinematic, a medium-wide shot frames...\n```\n\n### 4.2 Shots and Cuts\n\nDo not add a timestamp to the first shot. Use sequential shot numbers for later shots, and begin each one with a strictly increasing cut time that falls within the video duration:\n\n```text\n[Shot 2] At 00:03.500, the camera cuts to...\n```\n\nFor ordinary cuts, use `the camera cuts to`, `the shot cuts to`, `the shot transitions to`, `the shot changes to`, or `the shot switches to`. When explicitly requested by the user, cross-dissolve, fade, or wipe may also be used. A cut should introduce new information about the subject, space, state, viewpoint, or time. If only the distance or a slight angle needs to change, prefer camera motion.\n\n### 4.3 Camera Motion: Motion Type + Amplitude + Speed\n\nA complete camera-motion expression has three dimensions: the **motion type** defines how the camera moves, **amplitude** defines the range of compositional change, and **speed** defines the pacing of that change. Add amplitude and speed only when they are meaningful; medium amplitude and normal speed are usually omitted.\n\n| Dimension | Available Expression | Description |\n|-|-|-|\n| Motion type | `Zoom In / Zoom Out` | The focal length changes while the camera body remains stationary |\n| Motion type | `Push In / Pull Out` | The camera moves forward / backward |\n| Motion type | `Pan Left / Pan Right` | The camera remains in place while the lens pivots horizontally |\n| Motion type | `Truck Left / Truck Right` | The camera translates horizontally |\n| Motion type | `Tilt Up / Tilt Down` | The camera remains in place while the lens pivots vertically |\n| Motion type | `Pedestal Up / Pedestal Down` | The entire camera moves upward / downward |\n| Motion type | `Arc Shot` | The camera moves in an arc around the subject |\n| Motion type | `Tracking Shot` | The camera follows a moving subject |\n| Motion type | `Static Shot` | The camera position and lens remain still |\n| Motion type | `Shake Slightly / Shake Strongly` | Slight / strong camera shake |\n| Motion type | `POV` | The subject's point of view |\n| Motion type | `Roll Clockwise / Roll Counterclockwise` | The camera rolls clockwise / counterclockwise around the lens axis |\n| Amplitude | `with small amplitude` | Small-range change |\n| Amplitude | `with large amplitude` | Large-range change |\n| Speed | `at slow speed` | Slow movement |\n| Speed | `at fast speed` | Fast movement |\n\nCamera motion should be written as a natural English action within the shot, rather than stacked as separate labels at the end of a sentence:\n\n```text\nThe camera pushes in with small amplitude at slow speed toward the folded letter in her hands.\nThe camera pans right with large amplitude at fast speed, revealing the open doorway.\nThe camera holds a static shot as the runner exits the frame.\n```\n\n### 4.4 Speakers, Dialogue, and Singing\n\nSubjects who speak, sing, or produce an off-screen human voice use stable IDs such as `(S1)` and `(S2)`. When multiple already-numbered speakers speak or sing together, use a compound ID such as `(S1,S2)`. A speaker keeps the same ID across shots; characters who never vocalize receive no speaker ID.\n\nWhen a speaker first appears, provide enough information from the visual and audio context to establish a stable identity, such as character type, age, gender, whether the person is on-screen, pitch, timbre, speaking rate, or accent. Place the speaker's identifying phrase, ID, action, and delivery outside `<d>`. Inside `<d>`, include only the language tag and the actual user-provided spoken content. Preserve every original word and punctuation mark verbatim; do not translate or rewrite them.\n\n```text\nThe young woman with a quiet, breathy voice (S1) says: <d>[English] I get off at the next station.</d>\nThe two children (S1,S2) shout together, <d>[English] Wait for us!</d>\n```\n\nFor voiceover, use the exact phrase `says in an off-screen voiceover`. Immediately after every voiceover `<d>` block, state that the corresponding on-screen character's lips remain closed:\n\n```text\nThe man (S1) says in an off-screen voiceover: <d>[English] I still remember that road.</d> while his lips remain completely closed.\n```\n\nWhen the same line of dialogue or lyrics crosses a cut, use `<scenetrans>` at the connecting points in both parts and explicitly state that the audio continues across the cut. Use `<cutoff>` when speech is truncated by the end of the video. Continuity may be expressed with `continues seamlessly across the cut`, `continues uninterrupted into the next shot`, `carries over from the previous shot`, or `remains audible across the transition`.\n\n### 4.5 On-Screen Text\n\nPlace any banner, sign, label, subtitle, or neon text that is actually visible on screen in English double quotation marks. Preserve the original text and punctuation verbatim, without translation.\n\n```text\nA red neon sign reading \"营业中\" glows above the doorway.\n```\n\n### 4.6 overall_soundscape\n\nUse 1–4 English sentences in one continuous paragraph to summarize the ambient sound, physical action sounds, and non-verbal human sounds across the full video, such as wind, rain, traffic, footsteps, fabric movement, impacts, breathing, laughter, or panting. Dialogue, singing, and diegetic music already belong in the multimodal description and should not be repeated here. Use `N/A` only when the user explicitly requests complete silence throughout the video.\n\n```text\noverall_soundscape: Steady rain taps against the café windows while low room ambience continues underneath. The entrance bell rings once, followed by wet footsteps and the soft scrape of a chair.\n```\n\n### 4.7 non_diegetic_music\n\nUse 1–3 English sentences to describe background music that the characters cannot hear and only the audience can hear. Focus on instrumentation, speed, rhythm, and dynamic changes; do not use abstract mood words or explain the emotional function of the score. Singing, instruments, radio, television, or phone music audible to the characters are diegetic events and should appear in the multimodal description. Use `N/A` when there is no non-diegetic music.\n\n```text\nnon_diegetic_music: Sparse piano notes at a slow tempo, joined by sustained low strings that gradually increase in volume before fading out.\n```\n\n### 4.8 Physical Action and Timeline Planning\n\nFor any action involving contact, manipulation, impact, movement, or a changing object state, write the timeline as a chain of observable physical states rather than a summary of intended results. Every timed block must include:\n\n```text\nstarting state -> visible action -> physical connection or motion path -> ending state\n```\n\nThe ending state of one block must be the starting state of the next. State the actor, the relevant body part, the contacted object or surface, and the visible result. Use separate intervals for multi-step actions such as picking up, removing, wiping, transferring, colliding, releasing, catching, landing, or putting something on. Give the camera enough framing to show the relevant contact point.\n\nFor hand-operated objects, specify the total number of arms and hands, each hand's role, and which hand remains attached to the object. If two hands perform different parts of one action, describe both roles explicitly. Keep the action in a region where both hands and the object are visible. Avoid simultaneous wording that gives the same hand two incompatible roles.\n\nDo not use negative prompts as a substitute for a missing action path. First write what touches what, how the object moves, and where the action ends; then use a short restriction list to exclude only the most likely failure modes. Allocate time to the physical complexity of the action rather than distributing equal time to story beats.\n\nBefore delivery, check that the timeline has no gaps, every block has a readable exit state, adjacent states agree, and no unrequested prop is introduced. This validation applies equally to a fight, a person using an object, a screen interaction, a vehicle movement, or an ordinary environmental action.\n\n## 5. Cases\n\n### Case 1: T2VA\n\nWith no reference image, construct the complete timeline directly from the text. You may add scene, character, action, and sound details that remain consistent with the user's intent.\n\n```text\nintegrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] Live-action, cinematic, a medium-wide shot frames a baker opening the shutters of a small street bakery before sunrise. The camera pushes in with small amplitude at slow speed as the middle-aged baker with a calm, slightly raspy voice (S1) places a fresh loaf on the wooden counter and says: <d>[English] First batch of the morning.</d> [Shot 2] At 00:05.000, the camera cuts to a close-up of steam rising from the sliced bread while the baker's final words carry over from the previous shot.\n\noverall_soundscape: Wooden shutters scrape open over a quiet street as trays clink softly inside the bakery. The doorbell rings once, followed by light footsteps and the crisp sound of bread being sliced.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: A soft acoustic-guitar pattern at a moderate tempo, joined by sparse upright-bass notes and a gentle fade at the end.\n```\n\n### Case 2: I2VA\n\nWrite the first-frame instruction first, then use the subject, composition, and scene in Picture 1 as the starting point of Shot 1 before describing how the scene continues to develop.\n\n```text\nFor the target video, at 0.00 seconds into the target video, <Picture 1> (from [Shot 1]) is fully referenced.\n\nintegrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] Live-action, cinematic, the young woman shown in <Picture 1> remains beside the rain-covered train window, preserving her appearance, clothing, seat position, and the carriage layout. The camera trucks right with small amplitude at slow speed as she lifts her gaze from the folded letter toward the passing city lights. Her reflection moves across the glass while the quiet, breathy young woman (S1) says: <d>[English] I get off at the next station.</d> She folds the letter along its existing crease.\n\noverall_soundscape: The train wheels produce a steady metallic rhythm beneath a low ventilation hum. Rain ticks against the window while paper rustles softly in her hands.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: Sustained cello notes at a slow tempo with widely spaced piano tones, gradually decreasing in volume.\n```\n\n### Case 3: FL2VA\n\nThe two images anchor the opening and ending respectively. The body should not repeat two static image descriptions; instead, it should supply the motion path that connects them. The following example is an eight-second single shot.\n\n```text\nHow the reference pictures align with the target video — Picture 1 (from Shot 1) aligns with the 0.00-second mark of the target video; Picture 2 (from Shot 1) aligns with the 8.00-second mark of the target video.\n\nintegrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] Live-action, cinematic, a rain-soaked cyclist begins in the position and framing established by Picture 1, holding a closed black umbrella beside a silver bicycle. The camera pulls out with small amplitude at slow speed as she releases the bicycle handle, raises the umbrella above her shoulder, and presses the runner upward until the canopy opens. Water rolls from the expanding fabric while she steps beneath it, rotates the handle into the final angle, and settles into the pose, spacing, and composition established by Picture 2 at the end of the shot.\n\noverall_soundscape: Rain falls steadily on the pavement, followed by the metallic click of the umbrella runner and the soft snap of the canopy opening. Water drips from the bicycle frame as distant traffic passes.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: N/A\n```\n\n### Case 4: L2VA\n\nThe image anchors only the final moment. First establish a compatible earlier state, then let the actions, object states, and composition gradually land on Picture 1 in the final shot. The following example is a six-second single shot.\n\n```text\nHow the reference pictures align with the target video — <Picture 1> (from [Shot 1]) aligns with the 6.00-second mark of the target video.\n\nintegrated_multimodal_description: [Shot 1] Live-action, cinematic, a close shot begins with an intact drinking glass near the edge of a dark wooden table, while the same hand and sleeve visible in <Picture 1> approach from the right. The camera pushes in with small amplitude at slow speed as the fingertips strike the rim. The glass tips, falls, and hits the floor with a sharp impact; cracks spread through it as fragments slide outward. Toward the end, the moving pieces lose momentum and settle into the exact broken arrangement, hand position, camera angle, lighting, and final composition established by <Picture 1>.\n\noverall_soundscape: Fingertips tap the glass before it scrapes across the tabletop, falls, and breaks with a sharp crash. Small fragments scatter and gradually stop sliding across the floor.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: A low electronic pulse at a slow tempo, ending immediately after the glass breaks.\n```\n";

export const REF_EN = "# Full-Reference Mode Rewrite Output Format Guide\n\nThis guide explains how rewrite outputs are organized and written in full-reference mode.\n\nThe official six-section envelope is required for new Ref2VA prompts. Natural\nparagraphs and event-timed beats belong inside detailed_description, not in\nplace of the envelope. Use English visual prose and preserve the original\nlanguage for dialogue, lyrics, and visible text. The operational reference-mode\nguide is maintained under skills/h3-ref2va-prompt-writing/references/ref-en.txt;\nboth guides use references/output-format.json as the field contract.\n\n**Description detail:** Match detail to the shot's complexity. Establish relevant\nopening facts once, then develop action, exact speech, listener reactions,\nmotivated camera work, and sound. Avoid plot summaries and reference catalogues.\n\nDuration is a planning reference unless explicitly fixed. Let complete speech,\nnatural pauses, handoffs, listener reactions and necessary actions determine\nthe effective time. Shorten oversized references as well as extending short\nones. First-frame composition, appearance and already-completed opening actions\nare states, not extra timed events; description length is not playback time.\nA long silent hold needs a supported dramatic or visual purpose. Neither a\nreference nor a text estimate alone proves infeasibility.\nOverlap compatible lanes; reflow flexible windows\nwithout changing dialogue or events. Update the total and endpoints together.\nAt the caller's single-clip budget, split at complete turns or action results in\nplanning, or report the need to split in prompt-only work. Never rush or truncate.\n\n> The basic formats for shots, camera movement, speakers, dialogue, and ordinary sound are shared with the Video Prompt Writing Guide (T2VA / I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA). This guide focuses on the reference labels, analysis sections, and format differences specific to full-reference mode.\n\n## 1. Overall Structure\n\nUse these six fields once each, at the start of a line, in this exact order.\nEvery field has content; use N/A for absent ambience or music. Start\ndetailed_description with [Shot 1], and add consecutive shot labels only for\nactual internal cuts. Keep summary to task/reference scope, and retention_analysis\nto reference roles; neither repeats dialogue or the action timeline. The final\naction paragraph carries the approved ending. There is no mandatory eight-block\nchecklist and no character quota.\n\n| Section | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `subject_definitions` | Defines referenced content and its reference labels |\n| `summary` | Summarizes the task type, target video, and main reference relationships |\n| `retention_analysis` | Describes how referenced content is preserved, transferred, or reused |\n| `detailed_description` | Describes visuals, actions, shots, sound, and dialogue in playback order |\n| `overall_soundscape` | Summarizes ambience and physical sounds |\n| `non_diegetic_music` | Describes background music audible only to the audience |\n\n## 2. Reference Labels and Definitions (`subject_definitions`)\n\nFull-reference rewrites use four types of labels to identify the source and role of referenced content:\n\n| Label | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `<Subject N>` | Visible content abstracted from reference assets that can be reused or modified in the target video |\n| `<Picture N>` | A reference image used as a concrete target frame or shot-planning anchor |\n| `<Video N>` | A reference video that provides an editing source, continuation starting point, or whole-video temporal structure |\n| `<Audio N>` | An audio signal that is copied or referenced |\n\n> Once a reference label is assigned to a piece of content, it keeps the same meaning across `subject_definitions`, `summary`, `retention_analysis`, `detailed_description`, and the audio sections.\n\n`subject_definitions` defines each piece of referenced content that must be tracked separately later, such as a person, an environment, a source video's structure, or an audio track. Give each item its own line and explain what its label denotes, its reference role, and the main features to follow; name the corresponding source asset when its provenance needs to be made explicit. If `<Picture N>` or `<Video N>` only identifies the source of another referenced item and will not be analyzed or used separately later, cite it inside that item's definition without adding a separate line. `retention_analysis` records where each referenced item appears and whether it is fully preserved, partially preserved, transferred, or reused.\n\n### 2.1 `<Subject N>`\n\n`<Subject N>` is used for reusable visible content, including:\n\n- People, animals, or objects\n- Scenes, backgrounds, or environments\n- Clothing, props, interfaces, or visual effects\n- Styles, actions, expressions, or poses\n\nIt represents a content unit that will actually be used in the target video, rather than the source file itself. One subject may be defined by multiple reference assets, and one reference asset may provide multiple subjects.\n\n```text\n<Subject 1> is the young woman in <Picture 1>, with long dark hair, a blue cardigan, and a thin silver necklace.\n```\n\nWhen the same subject comes from multiple assets, combine the sources and state what each asset provides:\n\n```text\n<Subject 1> is the woman whose appearance comes from <Picture 1> and whose walking motion comes from <Video 1>.\n```\n\n### 2.2 `<Picture N>`\n\nUse a standalone `<Picture N>` when the reference image itself serves as a shot's first frame, keyframe, last frame, edited keyframe, or composition anchor:\n\n```text\n<Picture 2> is the first frame of [Shot 1], showing a woman seated beside a café window.\n```\n\nIf an image is used only to define a character, scene, costume, or style, do not create a standalone picture entry. Instead, cite the image source inside the corresponding `<Subject N>` definition.\n\nWhen an image acts as a storyboard or shot-planning reference, state which shots it maps to and what planning information it provides:\n\n```text\n<Picture 3> is a storyboard reference for [Shot 1] and [Shot 2], defining their viewpoint, subject placement, and shot order.\n```\n\n### 2.3 `<Video N>`\n\n`<Video N>` is reserved for whole-video relationships, such as:\n\n- Editing an original video\n- Continuing from the end of an original video\n- Referencing the original video's camera movement, cuts, rhythm, or temporal structure\n\n```text\n<Video 1> is the source video for the target video edit.\n```\n\nIf a person, object, scene, action, or effect from a reference video is reused as visible content, it still belongs under `<Subject N>`. `<Video N>` identifies the asset or structural source and does not replace subject labels.\n\n### 2.4 `<Audio N>`\n\n`<Audio N>` represents a standalone audio asset or an enabled synchronized audio track from a reference video. Common uses include:\n\n- Copying all or part of an audio signal\n- Referencing a background-music style\n- Referencing a speaker's voice timbre and delivery\n- Using dialogue, lyrics, or sound effects from the original audio\n- Referencing beat, rhythm, or audio continuity\n\nWhen an `<Audio N>` explicitly corresponds to a target speaker, reuse that speaker's global ID in the definition: write `<Subject N> (Sx)` when the speaker maps to a defined subject, or use a stable voice description followed by `(Sx)` otherwise. The ID comes from the target video's global speaker order and is not independently assigned or renumbered in the audio definition. See Section 5.4 for the speaker-numbering rules:\n\n```text\n<Audio 1> is the voice-timbre reference for <Subject 1> (S1).\n```\n\nWhen one audio asset serves multiple roles, describe those roles in one natural sentence rather than creating additional subsections.\n\n### 2.5 Visual and Audio Tracks from the Same Reference Video\n\n`<Video N>` and `<Audio N>` are numbered independently. Each index indicates only the label's order within its own category and does not encode a pairing between the two categories. The same reference video may therefore correspond to `<Video 1>` and `<Audio 2>`; different indices do not prevent them from coming from the same source asset.\n\nAn ordinary reference video does not create `<Audio N>` merely because the file contains sound.\n\nAn `<Audio N>` definition primarily states the audio's role and does not have to name the `<Video N>` it comes from. State the shared source only when needed to remove provenance ambiguity, for example:\n\n```text\n<Video 1> is the source video for the target video edit.\n<Audio 2> is the synchronized audio track of <Video 1> and is reused in the target video.\n```\n\n## 3. `summary`\n\nThis section uses one short English paragraph to summarize the target video and its reference relationships. It begins with a square-bracketed task-type prefix:\n\n```text\n[reference generation] ...\n[video editing + reference generation + audio reuse] ...\n```\n\nChoose task types according to the actual role each reference asset plays in the target video:\n\n| Task type | When to use it |\n| --- | --- |\n| `keyframe completion` | An image serves as the target video's first frame, keyframe, last frame, edited keyframe, or another concrete frame anchor |\n| `reference generation` | An image, video, or audio asset provides generation guidance for a character, scene, style, action, camera movement, storyboard, and so on, without serving as a concrete frame or as the source video being edited or continued |\n| `video editing` | An existing source video is directly modified; editing an image or generating between still keyframes does not belong to this type |\n| `video continuation` | New content continues, extends, resumes, or transitions from an existing source video |\n| `audio reuse` | The same audio signal is reused in full or in part |\n| `audio reference` | The audio signal is not copied directly; only its music style, timbre, dialogue or lyric content, sound-effect texture, beat, or continuity is referenced |\n\nWhen a task satisfies multiple relationships, combine the task types with ` + ` and do not repeat a type. For example, continuing from a source video while using an image as the last frame is written as `[video continuation + keyframe completion]`. Editing a source video while retaining its original audio may be written as `[video editing + audio reuse]`.\n\nThe mere presence of video or audio does not automatically create a corresponding task type. If a reference video provides only camera movement, cuts, or rhythm, it normally belongs to `reference generation`. Use `video editing` or `video continuation` only when that video is directly edited or continued.\n\nWhen editing a source video, use `audio reuse` as well if its original audio remains audible. When continuing a source video without directly copying the audio signal, use `audio reference` if the new audio only continues the original track's audible characteristics.\n\nThe summary uses the previously defined `<Subject N>`, `<Picture N>`, `<Video N>`, and `<Audio N>` labels to describe the main subjects, shot flow, and roles of the reference assets. Do not introduce new reference labels in this section.\n\nFor video-editing tasks, begin the summary after the task-type prefix with:\n\n```text\nThe target video is an edited version of <Video 1>.\n```\n\n## 4. `retention_analysis`\n\nThis section describes how each piece of referenced content is preserved, transferred, copied, or referenced in the target video. Use one line for each reference label and preserve the meaning established in `subject_definitions`.\n\n### 4.1 Visible Content\n\n`<Subject N>`, `<Picture N>`, and `<Video N>` use the following relationship markers. These markers are fixed English values in the output format:\n\n| Relationship marker | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fully_preserved` | The defined role of the referenced content is fully preserved |\n| `partially_preserved` | The referenced content is still used, but some defined characteristics are changed or only partially retained |\n| `attribute_transfer` | Referenced characteristics are transferred to a different identifiable target subject |\n| `weak_reference` | Only broad similarity in style, category, composition, or atmosphere is retained |\n\nSubject entry:\n\n```text\n<Subject 1> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 3]): fully_preserved - ...\n```\n\nPicture entry:\n\n```text\n<Picture 2> ([Shot 1] first frame): fully_preserved - ...\n```\n\nVideo-structure entry:\n\n```text\n<Video 1> (cut and pacing structure): weak_reference - ...\n```\n\n### 4.2 Audio\n\n`<Audio N>` uses the following relationship markers:\n\n| Relationship marker | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `fully_copy` | The complete source audio serves as the target video's complete final audio track |\n| `partially_copy` | Only part of the timeline or selected audio layers are copied, or other sounds are added, removed, or replaced after copying |\n| `reference` | The signal is not copied directly; only timbre, rhythm, music style, dialogue content, or sound texture is referenced |\n| `weak_reference` | Only broad similarity in category or atmosphere is retained |\n\n```text\n<Audio 1>: fully_copy - <Audio 1> is reused 1:1 as the target video's complete final audio track.\n```\n\n```text\n<Audio 2>: reference - the target speaker follows <Audio 2>'s voice timbre and measured delivery without copying the original signal.\n```\n\nChoose each relationship marker only within the reference role already defined for that label in `subject_definitions`. Do not treat newly added actions, backgrounds, or plot events in the target video as losses of reference fidelity.\n\n## 5. `detailed_description`\n\nThis is the main body of a full-reference rewrite. It describes visuals, actions, sound, and dialogue shot by shot in target-video playback order and inserts reference labels where they apply.\n\n### 5.1 Basic Format\n\nThe basic format follows the Video Prompt Writing Guide (T2VA / I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA):\n\n- Write the body in English. Preserve the original language of dialogue, lyrics, and visible text.\n- `[Shot 1]` marks the opening shot and has no timestamp. Later shots use `[Shot N] At MM:SS.mmm, ...` to mark cut times.\n- Write camera movement as natural English within the current shot, including movement type, amplitude, and speed when they need to be expressed.\n- Give vocal sources stable `(S1)`, `(S2)`, and subsequent IDs. Write dialogue and lyrics as `<d>[Language] ...</d>`.\n- Use `<scenetrans>`, `<cutoff>`, and the corresponding continuity descriptions for dialogue crossing a cut, speech truncated by the video ending, and continuous audio across shots.\n\nFor complete rules and examples covering camera vocabulary, group speech, voice-over, dialogue across cuts, and visible text, see the Video Prompt Writing Guide (T2VA / I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA).\n\n### 5.2 Full-Reference Mode Differences\n\n| Dimension | T2VA | Full-reference mode |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Main field | `integrated_multimodal_description` | `detailed_description` |\n| Style opening | Written after `[Shot 1]` | Established in one or two English sentences before `[Shot 1]` |\n| Reference information | Does not use full-reference labels | Inserts `<Subject N>`, `<Picture N>`, `<Video N>`, and `<Audio N>` at their first appearance and where their roles apply |\n| Audio relationships | Describes the target video's own sound | Cites `<Audio N>` in the corresponding shot or audio phase and states whether the signal is copied or referenced |\n\nOpening example:\n\n```text\nThe target video is in a cinematic, literary music-video style with soft lighting and a slightly desaturated color palette.\n[Shot 1] The scene opens in a crowded urban street...\n[Shot 2] At 00:09.000, the shot cuts to an extreme close-up...\n```\n\nFor generation tasks, `detailed_description` is normally 350-500 English words. Dialogue-dense content prioritizes fitting the complete spoken timeline rather than mechanically reaching a word count. Video-editing descriptions scale with the complexity of the source video and do not have to follow the generation-task range. A single shot does not automatically justify a shorter description; distribute detail across multiple shots according to their information load.\n\n### 5.3 Using Reference Labels in Shots\n\nAt the first clear appearance of an important `<Subject N>`, describe its referenced characteristics, position in the frame, and current action within what is actually visible in the shot. Continue using the same label in later shots without redefining what the label represents.\n\nUse natural phrasing for concrete frame anchors:\n\n```text\nthe shot begins from <Picture 1>\nthe shot's keyframe corresponds to <Picture 2>\nthe shot ends on <Picture 3>\n```\n\nWhen editing or continuing an original video, cite `<Video N>` naturally where its source state, structure, or continuation relationship applies. Cite `<Audio N>` in the shot or semantic phase where the audio relationship is active.\n\n### 5.4 Speakers, Audio Sources, and Dialogue\n\nThe basic speaker-ID and `<d>` formats follow T2VA. When a referenced subject physically speaks, retain both the visual reference label and the speaker ID:\n\n```text\n<Subject 2> (S1) turns toward the woman and says, <d>[English] Last summer, I went to my grandfather's house. He talked about you.</d>\n```\n\n`<Subject N>` identifies the referenced subject, while `(Sx)` identifies the actual speaker. When the subject speaks, write `<Subject N> (Sx)`. If the same subject speaks off-screen, keep the same form and mark it as `off-screen`. When the speaker does not correspond to a defined subject, use a stable voice description followed by `(Sx)`.\n\nWhen verbal content is only a cue within a directly reused BGM or complete soundtrack, and no person, character, narrator, or other independent vocal source physically produces it, use `<Audio N>` as the audible source and do not invent an additional `(Sx)`. If a concrete person, character, narrator, or other independent vocal source produces the voice, assign and reuse `(Sx)` for that source:\n\n```text\nWhen <Audio 1> reaches the phrase <d>[English] I'm lonely lonely lonely lonely lonely I'm lonely</d>, <Subject 1> performs the corresponding hand gesture without becoming a separate speaker source.\n```\n\nWhen dialogue, narration, or lyrics from reference audio are directly reused, or when the input prompt explicitly requests their reperformance, preserve the exact source words and original language inside `<d>`. Write `[unclear]` for unintelligible spans instead of guessing or paraphrasing them. Standardize punctuation to the basic written marks needed to express the sentence, such as `,`, `.`, `?`, and `!`; remove repeated tildes, emoji, bullets, and repeated or decorative punctuation. End complete statements, questions, and exclamations with `.`, `?`, or `!` respectively before `</d>`.\n\nWhen only timbre, rhythm, emotion, or delivery is referenced, do not carry the original dialogue from the reference audio into the target video.\n\nAssign `(Sx)` once according to the order of actual vocal events in the target video. Reuse the corresponding ID at every actual vocal event in `detailed_description`; an `<Audio N>` definition bound to a target speaker in `subject_definitions` also reuses the same `(Sx)` but never assigns a new one independently. Do not write `(Sx)` in `retention_analysis`. Verbal cues that exist only within a directly reused BGM or complete soundtrack use `<Audio N>`; voices physically produced by a concrete person, character, narrator, or other independent vocal source use `(Sx)`.\n\n## 6. `overall_soundscape` and `non_diegetic_music`\n\nThe definitions of these two sound categories follow the Video Prompt Writing Guide (T2VA / I2VA / FL2VA / L2VA).\n\n`overall_soundscape` summarizes ambience and physical sounds across the full video. Dialogue, singing, and sound events synchronized to a particular shot remain in `detailed_description`:\n\n```text\noverall_soundscape: Quiet indoor room tone and a low ventilation hum continue throughout the video.\n```\n\n`non_diegetic_music` describes background music that the characters cannot hear and that is audible only to the audience. When music is present, state its instrumentation, tempo, and dynamic development:\n\n```text\nnon_diegetic_music: A restrained solo-piano score at a slow tempo, with sustained low cello underneath and no swell.\n```\n\nWhen reference audio is used, state its copy or reference relationship only in the section that matches the audible layer: ambience and sound effects belong in `overall_soundscape`, while audience-only score belongs in `non_diegetic_music`. If the same audio provides both kinds of content, describe the corresponding relationship in each section:\n\n```text\noverall_soundscape: The copied ambience layer from <Audio 1> continues throughout the target video.\nnon_diegetic_music: <Audio 2> is directly reused as the complete audience-only score.\n```\n\nWrite complete dialogue and lyrics only inside `<d>` in `detailed_description`; do not repeat them in these two sections.\n\n## 7. Complete Example\n\n<details>\n<summary>Show the complete example</summary>\n\n```text\nsubject_definitions:\n<Subject 1> is the coffee-shop environment in <Picture 1>, featuring an exposed brick wall, an orange tufted sofa with patterned pillows, a neon sign, and a wooden coffee table.\n<Subject 2> is the fluffy white Samoyed in <Picture 2>, <Picture 3>, and <Picture 4>, with thick white fur, pointed ears, a dark nose, and a curved tail.\n<Subject 3> is the young blonde woman in <Video 1>, with long blonde hair and a light-pink button-down shirt with rolled-up sleeves.\n<Subject 4> is the young man in <Video 2>, with short wavy brown hair and a dark-grey hoodie with drawstrings.\n<Audio 1> is the voice-timbre reference for <Subject 3> (S1), containing a spoken English vocal layer.\n\nsummary:\n[reference generation + audio reference] The target video shows <Subject 3> eating a cookie in <Subject 1>. <Subject 4> enters with <Subject 2>, which lunges toward the cookie. The three-shot exchange uses <Audio 1> as the voice-timbre reference for <Subject 3> and ends with a canned audience laugh.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3]): fully_preserved - the exposed brick wall, orange tufted sofa, patterned pillows, neon sign, and wooden coffee table are retained.\n<Subject 2> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2]): fully_preserved - the Samoyed's thick white fur, pointed ears, dark nose, and curved tail are retained.\n<Subject 3> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2], [Shot 3]): fully_preserved - the blonde woman's identity, long hair, and light-pink shirt are retained.\n<Subject 4> (appears in [Shot 1], [Shot 2]): fully_preserved - the young man's short wavy brown hair and dark-grey hoodie are retained.\n<Audio 1>: reference - its vocal timbre guides the dialogue delivery of <Subject 3> without copying the original signal.\n\ndetailed_description:\nThe target video uses a realistic multi-camera sitcom style with warm indoor lighting.\n[Shot 1] A medium shot establishes <Subject 1>, the coffee shop with its exposed brick wall, orange tufted sofa, patterned pillows, neon sign, and wooden coffee table. <Subject 3> (S1), the young woman with long blonde hair and a light-pink button-down shirt with rolled-up sleeves, sits on the sofa holding a chocolate-chip cookie. From the left, <Subject 4>, the young man with short wavy brown hair and a dark-grey hoodie with drawstrings, enters holding the leash of <Subject 2>, the thick-furred white Samoyed with pointed ears, a dark nose, and a curved tail. The dog lunges toward the cookie and pulls the leash taut. <Subject 3> (S1) jerks her hand back and, using the clear youthful voice timbre referenced from <Audio 1>, exclaims with light annoyance, <d>[English] Hey! Watch your dog!</d> She closes her lips and guards the cookie while <Subject 4> pulls the dog back.\n[Shot 2] At 00:03.000, the shot cuts to a close-up of <Subject 4> (S2), the young man in the dark-grey hoodie from Shot 1, sitting beside <Subject 3> on the sofa and holding <Subject 2> securely in his arms. <Subject 4> (S2) says in a casual young male voice with a playful tone and an easy conversational pace, <d>[English] He just likes cookies more than me.</d> He closes his mouth into an apologetic smile and strokes the dog's thick white fur.\n[Shot 3] At 00:05.000, the shot cuts to a close-up of <Subject 3> (S1), the blonde woman in the light-pink shirt from Shot 1. Her annoyance softens as she looks toward the Samoyed. <Subject 3> (S1) replies in the same clear youthful voice referenced from <Audio 1> with an amused cadence, <d>[English] Well, he has good taste at least.</d> She smiles and raises the cookie in a small toast-like gesture. A classic canned audience laugh begins immediately after the line and continues through the final frame.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nSoft indoor coffee-shop room tone continues throughout the scene.\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nN/A\n```\n\n</details>\n";

export const OUTPUT_FORMAT = "{\n  \"version\": 1,\n  \"baseFields\": [\n    \"integrated_multimodal_description\",\n    \"overall_soundscape\",\n    \"non_diegetic_music\"\n  ],\n  \"referenceFields\": [\n    \"subject_definitions\",\n    \"summary\",\n    \"retention_analysis\",\n    \"detailed_description\",\n    \"overall_soundscape\",\n    \"non_diegetic_music\"\n  ],\n  \"baseTimelineField\": \"integrated_multimodal_description\",\n  \"referenceTimelineField\": \"detailed_description\",\n  \"shotLabel\": \"[Shot N]\",\n  \"emptyAudioValue\": \"N/A\"\n}\n";
